WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.591
Every loop iteration needs context, but unlimited context is neither possible nor desirable.

2
00:00:05.591 --> 00:00:11.611
Large, poorly selected histories can bury the current task, preserve outdated assumptions, increase cost,

3
00:00:11.611 --> 00:00:15.052
and make the model attend to irrelevant material.

4
00:00:15.052 --> 00:00:22.362
Context construction is an engineering decision about the information needed for the next action and the material

5
00:00:22.362 --> 00:00:24.943
that should stay outside the call.

6
00:00:24.943 --> 00:00:27.523
It helps to separate information layers.

7
00:00:27.523 --> 00:00:30.533
Stable task instructions define policy and boundaries.

8
00:00:30.533 --> 00:00:32.253
Structured state records progress.

9
00:00:32.253 --> 00:00:34.404
Recent observations capture tool results.

10
00:00:34.404 --> 00:00:36.984
Source artifacts provide authoritative project facts.

11
00:00:36.984 --> 00:00:40.424
Working notes may help with the current hypothesis.

12
00:00:40.424 --> 00:00:43.435
Durable memory can preserve lessons across runs.

13
00:00:43.435 --> 00:00:49.885
Archived history supports investigation, but it does not need to be copied into every iteration.

14
00:00:49.885 --> 00:00:54.186
Keeping these layers distinct makes context smaller and authority clearer.

15
00:00:54.186 --> 00:00:58.916
Summarization and compaction are useful when they preserve the right things.

16
00:00:58.916 --> 00:01:03.217
A good summary records decisions, evidence, unresolved questions, changed artifacts,

17
00:01:03.217 --> 00:01:06.657
and the current status while removing repetitive conversation.

18
00:01:06.657 --> 00:01:15.688
A poor summary can omit a constraint, blur a failed hypothesis into a fact, or lose the source of an observation.

19
00:01:15.688 --> 00:01:20.849
Critical facts should remain linked to source records that can be reread.

20
00:01:20.849 --> 00:01:26.439
The summary should reduce noise without becoming the only copy of the evidence.

21
00:01:26.439 --> 00:01:30.310
Provenance tells the loop where a fact came from.

22
00:01:30.310 --> 00:01:35.040
A user instruction, configuration file, tool result, test output, retrieved document,

23
00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:38.911
and model inference should not carry the same authority.

24
00:01:38.911 --> 00:01:45.791
If a log file contains text that looks like an instruction, it remains untrusted task data.

25
00:01:45.791 --> 00:01:52.672
If a model inferred a cause, that inference needs evidence before it drives a risky action.

26
00:01:52.672 --> 00:01:55.252
Progress tracking also needs freshness checks.

27
00:01:55.252 --> 00:02:01.703
Files, services, databases, and external systems may change between iterations or after a paused run.

28
00:02:01.703 --> 00:02:09.444
Before acting on old information, the loop may need to reread a file or rerun a targeted check.

29
00:02:09.444 --> 00:02:17.615
In the coding loop, the next call may need the task contract, failing test, related source file, latest patch,

30
00:02:17.615 --> 00:02:21.485
test output, and a short record of failed hypotheses.

31
00:02:21.485 --> 00:02:25.356
Useful memory supports progress without pretending continuity is perfect.
